.TH NCTOOL 1 "2026-03-11" "nctool 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nctool \- a simple naming convention tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nctool
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR COMMAND " [" "COMMAND OPTIONS" "] [" "COMMAND ARGUMENTS" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nctool
searches directories for files and directories that match or do not match
a given naming convention. It supports both searching for individual
matches and analyzing the overall adherence of a directory to a convention.
.PP
Standalone options
.RB ( \-\-help " and " \-\-version )
must be the only arguments supplied. This is also the case for standalone
options of commands (e.g.\&
.BR "nctool search \-\-help" ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version of
.B nctool
and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS search
.BI "nctool search [" OPTIONS "] [" CONVENTION "] [" LOCATION ]
.PP
Searches a directory for files and directories that match a naming
convention. By default, matching entries are printed. Options must precede
the convention and location arguments.
.PP
A search is only performed on the contents within the specified location.
For example,
.B nctool search pascalcase ~/Documents/
would not output
.IR Documents ,
even though it is a pascalcase match.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the search help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-full\-path
Display discovered files and directories as full paths.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-non\-matches
Display entries that do not match the convention instead of those that do.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-strict
Enforce the naming convention strictly (see
.BR "STRICT VS.\& LENIENT" ).
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Recursively search through subdirectories.
.SS analyze
.BI "nctool analyze [" OPTIONS "] [" CONVENTION "] [" LOCATION ]
.PP
Displays the percentage of files and directories that follow a naming
convention in a directory.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the analyze help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-strict
Enforce the naming convention strictly (see
.BR "STRICT VS.\& LENIENT" ).
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Analyze all subdirectories recursively.
.SH CONVENTIONS
The following naming conventions are supported. Each is shown with an
example filename.
.PP
.TS
tab(;);
lb lb
l l.
Convention;Example
flatcase;examplefilename.txt
camelcase;exampleFileName.txt
pascalcase;ExampleFileName.txt
snakecase;example_file_name.txt
constantcase;EXAMPLE_FILE_NAME.txt
kebabcase;example\-file\-name.txt
cobolcase;EXAMPLE\-FILE\-NAME.txt
.TE
.SH LOCATION
The location argument accepts absolute, variable, and relative paths to
directories.
.PP
.nf
/var/lib/        Absolute path.
~/Documents/     Variable path.
\&../Homework/     Relative path.
.fi
.SH STRICT VS.\& LENIENT
The default enforcement of naming conventions is lenient. Using the
.B \-s
or
.B \-\-strict
option enforces the convention strictly.
.PP
Strict enforcement means that the naming convention must be exhibited in
its entirety. Lenient enforcement means that the name could satisfy the
convention if more text were added, but not removed or changed.
.PP
.B Example:
.I example.txt
.TP
.B Matches strictly:
.BR flatcase " \- exactly matches the convention in its entirety."
.TP
.B Matches leniently:
.BR kebabcase " \- could be extended to " example\-file.txt .
.br
.BR snakecase " \- could be extended to " example_file.txt .
.br
.BR camelcase " \- could be extended to " exampleFile.txt .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure (invalid arguments, missing files, permission errors, etc.).
.SH EXAMPLES
Search for flatcase files in the current directory:
.PP
.nf
nctool search flatcase ./
.fi
.PP
Recursively search for non-matching snakecase files, showing full paths:
.PP
.nf
nctool search \-fnR snakecase /home/user/projects/
.fi
.PP
Analyze how strictly kebabcase a directory is:
.PP
.nf
nctool analyze \-sR kebabcase ~/Documents/
.fi
